Eventually, Jackson received a parcel containing a photograph of the fan, a gun, and a letter instructing him to die at a particular time. The fan would do the same once she had killed "their" baby, so they could be together in the "next life".  The Jacksons later discovered that the fan had been sent to a psychiatric hospital.{{sfn|Taraborrelli|2004|pp=223–224}}

sections.wikitext:
Jackson said he felt "Billie Jean" would be a success as he was writing it: "A musician knows hit material. Everything has to feel in place. It fulfills you and it makes you feel good. That's how I felt about 'Billie Jean'."{{sfn|Jackson|1988|pp=192–194}}<ref name="Blender"/> He said that, hearing it in his head while in his car, he was so absorbed that he did not realize his car had caught fire until a passing motorcyclist informed him.{{sfn|Jackson|1988|pp=192–194}}<ref name="Blender"/>

Jackson disagreed with the producer, [[Quincy Jones]], about the song. According to some reports, Jones felt it was too weak to be included on ''[[Thriller (Michael Jackson album)|Thriller]]'', but Jones denied this.{{sfn|Taraborrelli|2004|pp=223–224}}<ref>{{cite magazine|first= Lars |last= Brandle |title= Quincy Jones on Michael Jackson's 'Xscape': 'It's About Money' |magazine= [[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |date= May 21, 2014 |access-date= March 13, 2010 |url= https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/quincy-jones-on-michael-jacksons-xscape-its-about-money-6092238/}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|first= Susan |last= Daly |title= The genius of Jacko |date= March 21, 2009 |newspaper= [[Irish Independent]] |access-date= March 13, 2010 |url= http://www.independent.ie/entertainment/music/the-genius-----of-jacko-1681301.html}}</ref> Jones disliked the demo and did not care for the bassline,{{sfn|Halstead|2007|pp=36–37}} and wanted to cut Jackson's 29-second introduction.<ref name="Blender">{{cite magazine|title= The 500 Greatest Songs Since You Were Born: Number 1 |magazine= [[Blender (magazine)|Blender]] |date= October 2005 |url= http://www.blender.com/guide/articles.aspx?ID=1777 |url-status= dead |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20051215132835/http://www.blender.com/guide/articles.aspx?ID=1777 |archive-date= December 15, 2005}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|first= Denise |last= Winterman |title= Thrills and spills and record breaks |work= [[BBC News Online]] |date= November 30, 2007 |access-date= March 13, 2010 |url= https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/magazine/7117000.stm}}</ref> Jackson, however, insisted that it be kept. According to Jones, he conceded when Jackson said it made him want to dance: "And when Michael Jackson tells you, 'That's what makes me want to dance', well, the rest of us just have to shut up."<ref name="Blender"/><ref>{{cite web|first= Molly |last= Glentzer |title= The steps that made Michael Jackson great |newspaper= [[Houston Chronicle]] |date= July 1, 2009 |access-date= March 13, 2010 |url= https://www.chron.com/disp/story.mpl/life/main/6507076.html}}</ref>

Jones also wanted to change the title to "Not My Lover", as he believed that people would think the song referred to the tennis player [[Billie Jean King]].<ref>{{cite news|title= Moonwalker everything everyone already knows about Michael Jackson is in his fluffy autobiography |newspaper= [[San Jose Mercury News]] |date= April 21, 1988 |access-date= March 13, 2010 |url= http://nl.newsbank.com/nl-search/we/Archives?p_product=SJ&s_site=mercurynews&p_multi=SJ&p_theme=realcities&p_action=search&p_maxdocs=200&p_topdoc=1&p_text_direct-0=0EB72D8FDF01B3B7&p_field_direct-0=document_id&p_perpage=10&p_sort=YMD_date:D&s_trackval=GooglePM |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20160305021158/http://nl.newsbank.com/nl-search/we/Archives?p_product=SJ&s_site=mercurynews&p_multi=SJ&p_theme=realcities&p_action=search&p_maxdocs=200&p_topdoc=1&p_text_direct-0=0EB72D8FDF01B3B7&p_field_direct-0=document_id&p_perpage=10&p_sort=YMD_date:D&s_trackval=GooglePM |archive-date= March 5, 2016}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|first= Michael |last= Jackson |title= Thriller of a Lifetime |newspaper= [[Toronto Star]] |date= May 1, 1988 |access-date= March 13, 2010 |url= https://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/thestar/access/473617611.html?dids=473617611:473617611&FMT=ABS&FMTS=ABS:FT&type=current&date=May+01%2C+1988&author=Michael+Jackson&pub=Toronto+Star&desc='Thriller+of+a+Lifetime'&pqatl=google |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20130210011434/http://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/thestar/access/473617611.html?dids=473617611:473617611&FMT=ABS&FMTS=ABS:FT&type=current&date=May+01%2C+1988&author=Michael+Jackson&pub=Toronto+Star&desc=%27Thriller+of+a+Lifetime%27&pqatl=google |archive-date= February 10, 2013 |ref= none}}</ref> Jackson refused to change the title and asked Jones to give him co-producing credits for the track, as he felt that the finished product sounded close to his demo. In addition, Jackson wanted extra royalties. Jones granted him neither and the two fell out for several days.{{sfn|Taraborrelli|2004|pp=223–224}}{{sfn|Halstead|2007|pp=36–37}}

The American composer [[Michael Boddicker]] said that Jackson played the synthesizer part in one take on the [[Yamaha CS-80]] in seven minutes.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Boddicker |first=Michael |date=September 1, 2009 |title=Michael Jackson Keyboard Sounds of His Signature Songs Then and Now: The Vintage Keys of Thriller |url=http://www.keyboardmag.com/artists/1236/michael-jackson-keyboard-sounds-of-his-signature-songs-then-and-now/27406 |magazine=[[Keyboard Magazine]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140705162028/http://www.keyboardmag.com/artists/1236/michael-jackson-keyboard-sounds-of-his-signature-songs-then-and-now/27406 |archive-date=July 5, 2014}}</ref> However, the synthesizer player Bill Wolfer disputed this, saying he played the CS-80 chords on both the demo and the album version, and that Boddicker was misremembering the session: "Everyone who worked with Michael Jackson knows that he didn't play keys but a tiny bit, and never played them on a record!"<ref name="MusicRadar-2022">{{Cite web |last= |date=2022-12-06 |title=Michael Jackson collaborator Bill Wolfer sets the record straight on 'Billie Jean' synth sound: "Anyone could have played that part, but I'm proud of the fact that it was my sound design" |url=https://www.musicradar.com/news/bill-wolfer-michael-jackson-billie-jean |access-date=2023-02-14 |website=[[MusicRadar]] |language=en}}</ref> According to Wolfer, the part was his sound design: he programmed it to form a hybrid of brass and string sounds, which had a vocal-like quality. Jackson heard Wolfer experimenting with the sound on the CS-80 during the [[Triumph Tour]] with [[the Jacksons]], which he liked and called Wolfer in for.<ref name="MusicRadar-2022" />

Engineer [[Bruce Swedien]] had Jackson sing his vocal overdubs through a six-foot cardboard tube.<ref name="Blender"/> Jackson's lead vocal was performed in one take; he had received vocal training every morning throughout the production of the song.{{sfn|Wadhams|2001|pp=418–422}} Jazz [[Saxophone|saxophonist]] [[Tom Scott (saxophonist)|Tom Scott]] played the [[lyricon]], an electronic wind instrument. Bassist [[Louis Johnson (bassist)|Louis Johnson]] played his part on every bass guitar he owned, before Jackson settled for a [[Yamaha Corporation|Yamaha]] bass.<ref name="Blender" />

Instructed by Jones to create a drum sound with "sonic personality" that no one had heard before, Swedien constructed a platform for the drum kit with special elements including a flat piece of wood between the snare and hi-hat. He said: "There aren't many pieces of music where you can hear the first three or four notes of the drums, and immediately tell what the piece of music is. But I think that is the case with 'Billie Jean'—and that I attribute to sonic personality."<ref name="Blender" />{{sfn|Halstead|2007|pp=36–37}} 

Swedien mixed the song 91 times—unusual for him, since he usually mixed a song just once. The mixes grew progressively worse, and Jones asked Swedien to listen again to the second mix, which was much better. The second mix was the final version.{{sfn|Halstead|2007|pp=36–37}}

