On November 30, 1982,<ref>{{cite magazine|first= Steve |last= Greenberg |title= Michael Jackson's 'Thriller' at 30: How One Album Changed the World |magazine= [[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |date= November 29, 2012 |access-date= December 26, 2016 |url= https://www.billboard.com/music/music-news/michael-jacksons-thriller-at-30-how-one-album-changed-the-world-473949/}}</ref> ''Thriller'' was released to critical and commercial success.{{sfn|Taraborrelli|2004|p=222}} On January 2, 1983, "Billie Jean" was released as the second single, after "[[The Girl Is Mine]]".{{sfn|Halstead|2007|pp=37–38}}{{sfn|George|2004|p=39}} It reached number one on the [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]] on March 5, 1983 and stayed there for seven weeks, becoming Jackson's biggest solo hit.<ref>{{cite magazine|first= Gary |last= Trust |title= Rewinding the Charts: In 1983, Michael Jackson's 'Billie Jean' Moonwalked to No. 1 on the Hot 100 |magazine= [[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |date= March 6, 2018 |access-date= July 4, 2020 |url= https://www.billboard.com/pro/michael-jackson-billie-jean-rewinding-the-charts-1983/}}</ref> ''Billboard'' ranked it as the number two song for 1983.<ref name="US"/> "Billie Jean" topped the [[Hot R&B/Hip-Hop Songs|R&B chart]] within three weeks, and became Jackson's fastest-rising number one single since "[[ABC (The Jackson 5 song)|ABC]]", "[[The Love You Save]]" and "[[I'll Be There (The Jackson 5 song)|I'll Be There]]" in 1970, which he recorded as a member of [[the Jackson 5]]. It remained at number one for nine weeks on the R&B chart, being preceded by [[the Gap Band]]'s "[[Outstanding]]", before the single was eventually succeeded by [[George Clinton (funk musician)|George Clinton]]'s "[[Atomic Dog]]".{{sfn|Halstead|2007|pp=37–38}} "Billie Jean" peaked at number nine on the [[Adult Contemporary (chart)|Adult Contemporary chart]].{{sfn|George|2004|p=39}} It was also number one in the [[UK singles chart]]. "Billie Jean" and ''Thriller'' topped both the singles and album charts in the same week on both sides of the Atlantic simultaneously, a feat few acts have achieved. The song was the third best-selling single of 1983 in the US and ninth in the UK.{{sfn|Halstead|2007|pp=37–38}} It also reached number one in Canada, Switzerland and Belgium, and the top ten in Austria, Italy, New Zealand, Norway and Sweden.<ref name="AUT"/><ref name="CAN"/> In 2026, after the release of the biographical film ''[[Michael (2026 film)|Michael]]'', the song re-entered the [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]] debuting at number 15 the week ending May 23, 2026, and also entered the [[Billboard Global 200|''Billboard'' Global 200]] chart for the first time, debuting at number one the week ending May 23, 2026, marking a historic milestone for Michael Jackson as the first artist who died before the creation of the Global 200 chart to reach number one.<ref name=global>{{cite web|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/chart-beat/michael-jackson-billie-jean-global-200-may-23-1236251023/|title=Michael Jackson’s ‘Billie Jean’ Thrills at No. 1 on Billboard Global 200|date=2026-05-18|work=Billboard|author=Trust, Gary|accessdate=2026-05-25}}</ref>

''[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]'' called "Billie Jean" a "brilliant piece of writing" and "one of [Jackson's] finest performances."<ref>{{cite magazine|magazine=Billboard|title=Top Single Picks|date=January 22, 1983|accessdate=2023-02-08|page=69|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=5iMEAAAAMBAJ}}</ref> In a ''[[Rolling Stone]]'' review, Christopher Connelly described it as a "lean, insistent funk number whose message couldn't be more blunt: 'She says I am the one/But the kid is not my son'". He added that the track was a "sad, almost mournful song, but a thumping resolve underlies [Jackson's] feelings".<ref name="RS">{{cite magazine|first= Christopher |last= Connelly |title= Rolling Stone's ''Thriller'' review |magazine= [[Rolling Stone]] |date= January 21, 1997 |url= https://www.rollingstone.com/artists/michaeljackson/albums/album/303823/review/6067536/thriller |url-status= dead |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20070205080932/http://www.rollingstone.com/artists/michaeljackson/albums/album/303823/review/6067536/thriller |archive-date= February 5, 2007}}</ref> ''[[Blender (magazine)|Blender]]'' stated that the song was "one of the most sonically eccentric, psychologically fraught, downright bizarre things ever to land on Top 40 radio". They added that it was "frighteningly stark, with a pulsing, cat-on-the-prowl bass figure, whip-crack downbeat and eerie multi-tracked vocals ricocheting in the vast spaces between keyboards and strings". Overall, the magazine described the track as "a five-minute-long nervous breakdown, set to a beat".<ref name="Blender"/> ''[[Stylus Magazine|Stylus]]'' said of the song, "It's one of the best representations of film noir in pop music, ending with no resolution except a single mother and selfish, careless scumball."<ref>{{cite web|first= Tal |last= Rosenberg |title= Michael Jackson – Thriller – The Diamond |website= [[Stylus Magazine]] |date= June 19, 2007 |access-date= February 21, 2009 |url= http://www.stylusmagazine.com/articles/diamond/michael-jackson-thriller.htm |archive-date= March 20, 2015 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20150320145345/http://stylusmagazine.com/articles/diamond/michael-jackson-thriller.htm}}</ref> In a review of ''[[Thriller 25]]'', [[AllMusic]] observed that "Billie Jean" was "startling" in its "futuristic funk".<ref name="AM">{{cite web|first= Stephen |last= Thomas Erlewine |author-link= Stephen Thomas Erlewine |title= The Funk of 25 Years: Thriller Hits the Quarter Century Mark |publisher= AllMusic |access-date= January 13, 2014 |url= https://www.allmusic.com/blog/post/the-funk-of-25-years-thriller-hits-the-quarter-century-mark}}</ref> The track also won praise from Jackson biographers. [[Nelson George]] stated that [[Jerry Hey]]'s string arrangement added danger to "Billie Jean", while J. Randy Taraborrelli added that it was "dark and sparse" by Quincy Jones' production standards.{{sfn|Taraborrelli|2004|pp=223–224}}{{sfn|George|2004|p=23}}

At the [[26th Annual Grammy Awards|1984 Grammy Awards]], "Billie Jean" earned Jackson two of a record eight awards: [[Grammy Award for Best R&B Song|Best R&B Song]] and [[Grammy Award for Best Male R&B Vocal Performance|Best R&B Male Vocal Performance]]. It won the [[Billboard Music Award]] for favorite dance/disco 12" LP, and the magazine's 1980's poll named "Billie Jean" as the "Black Single of the Decade". The [[American Music Awards]] recognized the track as the Favorite Pop/Rock Single, while ''[[Cashbox (magazine)|Cash Box]]'' honored the song with the awards for Top Pop Single and Top Black Single. The track was recognized with the Top International Single award by the Canadian Black Music Awards and awarded the Black Gold Award for Single of the Year. "Billie Jean" has also been awarded for its sales. It won the [[Music Business Association|National Association of Recording Merchandisers]] Gift of Music award for best-selling single in 1984. By 1989, the standard format single was [[music recording sales certification|certified]] platinum by the [[Recording Industry Association of America]], for shipments of at least one million units.{{sfn|Halstead|2007|pp=38–40}}{{sfn|George|2004|pp=39–43}} The [[Music download|digital sales]] of "Billie Jean" were certified gold in 2005, for shipments of at least 500,000 units.<ref name="RIAA">{{cite web|title= Gold & Platinum – Michael Jackson – Billie Jean |publisher= [[Recording Industry Association of America]] |access-date= May 13, 2016 |url= https://www.riaa.com/gold-platinum/?tab_active=default-award&ar=michael+jackson&ti=billie+jean}}</ref> In May 2014, a [[viral video]] of a high school-aged teenager imitating Jackson's ''Motown 25'' performance of the song helped the song re-enter the Billboard Hot 100 at number 14, with 95% of its chart points credited to streams of the song following the popularity of viral video.<ref>{{cite magazine|first1= Gary |last1= Trust |first2= Keith |last2= Caulfield |magazine= Billboard |title= Michael Jackson's 'Billie Jean' Returns to Hot 100; Legend Lands 50th Hit |date= May 28, 2014 |access-date= May 29, 2014 |url= https://www.billboard.com/pro/michael-jackson-billie-jean-hot-100-return-xscape/}}</ref> On August 29, 2022, the digital sales of "Billie Jean" were certified Diamond in US.<ref name="RIAA2018">{{cite news|title= Michael Jackson's Catalogue Garners Major New Gold & Platinum Awards |work= [[Recording Industry Association of America]] |date= August 23, 2018 |access-date= September 9, 2018 |url= https://www.riaa.com/michael-jacksons-catalogue-garners-major-new-gold-platinum-awards/}}</ref>

