{{main|Motown 25: Yesterday,  Today, Forever|l1=''Motown 25: Yesterday,  Today, Forever''}}

Jackson performed "Billie Jean" on the television special ''[[Motown 25: Yesterday, Today, Forever]]'', broadcast on May 15, 1983. The special was recorded on March 25 as a celebration of [[Motown|Motown Records]]' twenty-fifth anniversary (Motown was launched in 1959). The event featured many popular Motown acts, past and present. Jackson initially refused an invitation to reunite with the Jackson 5 for a performance, but reconsidered after a visit from Motown founder [[Berry Gordy]], whom Jackson respected. Jackson asked to also perform "Billie Jean", which ended up being the only non-Motown song on the show, to which Gordy agreed.{{sfn|Taraborrelli|2004|pp=234–240}}<ref name="Moonwalk">{{cite magazine|title= Inside Michael Jackson's Iconic First Moonwalk Onstage |magazine= Rolling Stone |date= October 5, 2015 |access-date= February 3, 2018 |url= https://www.rollingstone.com/music/news/inside-michael-jackson-s-iconic-first-moonwalk-onstage-20151005}}</ref> Following performances by [[Marvin Gaye]], [[Smokey Robinson]], and [[Mary Wells]], the Jacksons sang a medley of their early 1970s hits. [[Jermaine Jackson]] was on stage with the group, marking the first time that the original [[The Jackson 5|Jackson 5]] lineup had performed together since they left Motown in 1975. After the group performed "[[I'll Be There (The Jackson 5 song)|I'll Be There]]", they left Michael alone on stage.<ref>{{cite news|first= John J. |last= O'Connor |title= TV: Sound of Motown Celebrated |newspaper= [[The New York Times]] |date= May 16, 1983 |access-date= October 5, 2020 |url= https://www.nytimes.com/1983/05/16/arts/tv-sound-of-motown-celebrated.html}}</ref>

Jackson wore black [[Capri pants|high-water]] pants, leather [[Slip-on shoe|penny loafers]], [[crystal]]-laden white socks, a black [[sequin]]ed jacket, and a single white rhinestone glove. He addressed the audience, and then "Billie Jean" started playing. (Jackson lip-synced the entire song, because organizers feared that the evening's backing band could not replicate the sound of the recording.)<ref name="Moonwalk"/> To begin his performance, Jackson snapped a [[fedora]] to his head and struck a pose—his right hand on his hat and his left leg bent. During the song's instrumental interlude, he executed a move that many believe sealed his status as a [[pop icon]].<ref>{{cite news |first= Todd |last= Leopold |title= Michael Jackson: A life in the spotlight |publisher= [[CNN]] |date= June 6, 2005 |access-date= February 15, 2009 |url= http://edition.cnn.com/2005/SHOWBIZ/Music/01/30/jackson.life/ |archive-date= July 13, 2012 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20120713223358/http://edition.cnn.com/2005/SHOWBIZ/Music/01/30/jackson.life/ |url-status= dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|first= Bob |last= Cannon |title= A Giant Step for Jackson |magazine= [[Entertainment Weekly]] |date= March 25, 1994 |access-date= February 15, 2009 |url= https://ew.com/article/1994/03/25/encore-michael-jackson-moonwalks/ |url-status= live |archive-date= April 21, 2009 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20090421093636/http://www.ew.com/ew/article/0,,301524,00.html}}</ref> Jackson glided backwards to perform the [[Moonwalk (dance)|moonwalk]], before he spun on his heels and landed ''[[Pointe technique|en pointe]]''.{{sfn|Taraborrelli|2004|pp=234–240}} It was the first time Jackson had performed the moonwalk in public; he had practiced it in his kitchen prior to the show.{{sfn|Campbell|1993|pp=65–68}}

Many, including Jackson biographer Steve Knopper, have speculated that Jackson's "Billie Jean" costuming and choreography were specifically inspired by a dance routine performed by [[Bob Fosse]] in the 1974 musical film ''[[The Little Prince (1974 film)|The Little Prince]]''.<ref name="Moonwalk"/> In that film, Fosse, playing "The Snake", sings the song "A Snake in the Grass" and performs an accompanying dance that Fosse himself choreographed. The dance includes a variety of techniques that seemed to be echoed in Jackson's routine, including trademark Fosse elements such as hip thrusts, bent knees, shoulder isolations, [[jazz hands]] and kicks, as well as a brief moonwalk.<ref name="Moonwalk"/><ref name="Fosse">{{cite book|first= Sam |last= Wasson |year= 2013 |title= Fosse |publisher= Eamon Dolan/Houghton Mifflin Harcourt |page= [https://archive.org/details/fosse0000wass/page/533 533] |isbn= 978-0-54755-329-0 |url= https://archive.org/details/fosse0000wass |url-access= registration}}</ref> Jackson openly acknowledged Fosse as a dance influence, having told Fosse himself that he had amassed a complete home collection of Fosse's choreography work in film and television, which he watched frequently.<ref name="Fosse"/>

''Motown 25: Yesterday, Today, Forever'' was watched by 50 million people and Jackson's routine earned him an [[Emmy Award|Emmy]] nomination.<ref>{{cite news|title= A Glance at the Career of Michael Jackson |publisher= [[Fox News Channel]] |date= June 14, 2005 |access-date= February 15, 2009 |url= https://www.foxnews.com/story/a-glance-at-the-career-of-michael-jackson |archive-date= October 25, 2012 |url-status= live |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20121025011107/http://www.foxnews.com/story/0,2933,159509,00.html}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|first= Chris |last= Harris |title= Michael Jackson, Akon Are 'Startin' Somethin' on Thriller 25 Track |publisher= MTV |date= January 14, 2008 |access-date= February 15, 2009 |url= http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1579562/20080114/jackson_michael.jhtml?loc=interstitialskip|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20080121005540/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1579562/20080114/jackson_michael.jhtml?loc=interstitialskip|url-status= dead|archive-date= January 21, 2008}}</ref> With the performance, Jackson reached a new audience and increased the sales of ''Thriller'', which eventually became the best-selling album of all-time.{{sfn|Campbell|1993|pp=65–68}} The day after the show aired, Jackson was called by his childhood idol [[Fred Astaire]], who commended him. Another childhood idol, [[Sammy Davis Jr.]], had admired Jackson's black sequined jacket during the performance and later received it as a gift.{{sfn|Campbell|1993|pp=65–68}}

Jackson stated at the time that he was disappointed in his performance; he had wanted to remain on his toes longer than he had.{{sfn|Taraborrelli|2004|pp=234–240}} Jackson subsequently said that "Billie Jean" was one of his favorite songs to perform live, but only when he did not have to do it the way he had on ''Motown 25: Yesterday, Today, Forever''. "The audience wants a certain thing—I have to do the moonwalk in that spot," he later said. "I'd like to do a different version."{{sfn|Halstead|2007|pp=38–40}}

In a Top 100 list compiled by [[VH1]] and ''[[Entertainment Weekly]]'' in 2000, Jackson's performance was ranked as the sixth greatest [[Rock and roll|rock 'n' roll]] TV moment.<ref>{{cite news|title= Top Rock TV Moment A Really Big Show |work= [[CBS News]] |date= July 24, 2000 |access-date= February 15, 2009 |url= https://www.cbsnews.com/news/top-rock-tv-moment-a-really-big-show/}}</ref> Five years later, ''Entertainment Weekly'' named Jackson's ''Motown 25'' performance as one of the most important pop culture moments in history. "It was a moment that crossed over in a way that no live musical performance ever had. There was a messianic quality to it", ''Entertainment Weekly'' editor Steve Daly commented.<ref>{{cite news |title= The 25 Most Important Pop Culture Moments |publisher= CNN |date= September 1, 2005 |access-date= February 15, 2009 |url= http://edition.cnn.com/2005/US/09/01/cnn25.top25.entertainment/index.html |archive-date= October 21, 2012 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20121021025231/http://edition.cnn.com/2005/US/09/01/cnn25.top25.entertainment/index.html |url-status= dead }}</ref> David Moynihan of ''[[NME]]'' wrote in 2009 that "Jackson's status as the world's biggest superstar was confirmed" by the ''Motown 25'' performance.<ref>{{cite magazine|first= David |last= Moynihan |title= Michael Jackson: 1958-2009 – obituary |date= June 26, 2009 |magazine= [[NME]] |url= https://www.nme.com/news/music/michael-jackson-363-1304241}}</ref>

